    objectives: ["Explain why Jesus placed the source of sin in the heart", "Compare outward obedience with inward holiness", "Guard the heart through prayer, Scripture and honest confession"],
    terms: [["Heart", "In Scripture, the centre of a person's thoughts, desires and will."], ["Hypocrisy", "Appearing righteous outwardly while being corrupt within."], ["Defile", "To make unclean or unfit for God's presence."]],
    intro: "Many people believe they are good because they keep certain rules, attend church or avoid certain scandals. Jesus challenged that comfort. In a conversation about hand-washing, he said that nothing outside a person can defile him by going in, but what comes out of a person is what defiles. This lesson explores what that means for how we understand ourselves.",
    context: "The Pharisees and scribes criticised Jesus' disciples for eating with unwashed hands, breaking the traditions of the elders. Jesus replied by quoting Isaiah about people who honour God with their lips while their hearts are far from him. He then called the crowd and explained that sin comes from within. In the Sermon on the Mount he showed that the commandments against murder and adultery reach to anger and lust.",
    observe: "In Mark 7:21-22 Jesus lists thirteen evils that come from within: evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, coveting, wickedness, deceit, sensuality, envy, slander, pride and foolishness. In Matthew 5:22 he says that everyone who is angry with his brother is liable to judgment, and in verse 28 that whoever looks at a woman with lustful intent has already committed adultery in his heart.",
    points: [
      ["The root of sin is inside us", "Jesus taught that our actions flow out of the heart like fruit from a tree. Changing behaviour without changing the heart is like cutting weeds without removing the roots. Jeremiah 17:9 warns that the heart is deceitful above all things and desperately sick. This is why education, laws and good resolutions cannot fully solve the problem of sin."],
      ["God judges motives, not only actions", "Samuel learned that man looks on the outward appearance but the Lord looks on the heart. Jesus applied this to murder and adultery, showing that anger, contempt and lust are serious in God's eyes. This does not mean thoughts are as harmful as deeds, but it means no one can claim to be righteous simply because they have avoided certain outward acts."],
      ["We need a new heart, and we must guard the one we have", "Proverbs 4:23 says to keep your heart with all vigilance, for from it flow the springs of life. We guard it by feeding on Scripture, praying honestly, avoiding what stirs sinful desire and confessing quickly. But guarding is not enough. Ezekiel 36:26 promises that God will give a new heart, and this is what the gospel provides."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Why do people prefer to measure holiness by outward rules? What are the dangers of doing so?", "Outward rules are easy to see and control, and they let us feel safe. The danger is pride, hypocrisy and neglect of the heart. Ask learners for examples from church life."],
      ["Which item in Jesus' list in Mark 7:21-22 do you think is most commonly excused today?", "Pride, envy, slander and deceit are often treated lightly. Let learners respond honestly and avoid naming particular people."],
      ["How did Jesus apply the commandments about murder and adultery to the heart?", "He showed that anger and lust are rooted in the same rebellion. The law's purpose reaches deeper than behaviour."],
      ["What practical steps help to guard the heart each day?", "Daily Scripture, prayer, fellowship, careful use of media, quick confession. Encourage learners to name habits they can realistically keep."],
    ],
    apply: "Choose one attitude from Jesus' list that you struggle with, such as pride, envy or anger. Each morning this week, pray Psalm 139:23-24 and ask God to search your heart. Write down what he shows you and confess it, then ask for the Spirit's help to change.",
    activity: "Draw a tree on the board. Label the roots 'heart: thoughts and desires' and the fruit 'words and actions'. Ask learners to name fruits seen in daily life and trace each to a root. Read Mark 7:20-23 and discuss why pruning the fruit alone does not solve the problem.",
    takeHome: "Read Mark 7:1-23, Matthew 5:21-30 and Psalm 51:10. Write a short prayer asking God for a clean heart. List three practical habits that will help you guard your heart this month.",
    prayer: "Ask God to search our hearts. Confess hidden attitudes. Thank him for the promise of a new heart and ask him to renew ours.",
    teacher: "Be careful not to make learners anxious about every passing thought. Jesus is describing settled desires and attitudes that we nurse, not every temptation that crosses the mind. Point toward the grace that forgives and the Spirit who changes the heart.",
    assess: "Weekly check: ask learners why Jesus said sin comes from the heart and how to guard it.",
    mcq1: ["According to Mark 7:20, what defiles a person?", ["What comes out of him", "What he eats", "Where he lives", "Who his parents are"], 0, "Jesus said what comes out of a person defiles him."],
    mcq2: ["What does Proverbs 4:23 tell us to guard?", ["The heart", "The house", "The money", "The name"], 0, "Keep your heart with all vigilance."],
